Set up a stick from scratch — wipefs, partition table, filesystem
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Seven steps from any starting state to a clean stick:
  umount -> wipefs -a -> mklabel -> mkpart -> partprobe -> mkfs -> verify

USAGE
  usb format /dev/sdb --fs vfat --label DATA
  usb -s format /dev/sdb --fs exfat        (show only, run nothing)
  usb -y format /dev/sdb --fs vfat         (run without asking)

WHY wipefs AND mklabel?
  wipefs -a removes every signature anywhere on the stick — including those of
  an old ISO or a second, hidden partition table. mklabel then creates a fresh
  table. mklabel alone would leave old filesystem signatures behind, and some
  systems still find them.

WHY partprobe?
  After mkpart the kernel does not know /dev/sdb1 yet. Without partprobe the
  following mkfs fails with "No such file or directory".

msdos OR gpt?
  Without --table the recipe picks msdos for vfat and gpt otherwise.
  msdos (MBR) is understood by car radios, TVs and old BIOS machines.
  gpt is only needed beyond 2 TB — and some devices ignore gpt sticks entirely.

LABEL
  FAT32 stores labels uppercase only and at most 11 characters long. The recipe
  converts and truncates automatically and warns when it does. Without --label
  no label is set at all.

IT FORMATS THE PARTITION, NOT THE DEVICE
  mkfs runs on /dev/sdb1, not on /dev/sdb. A filesystem straight on the device
  does work on Linux, but Windows will consider the stick empty.
